    {"principal": "az-deploy-svc@corp.example.my", "inferred_role": "abused_service_principal_management_plane", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:11+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Service Discovery", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 08:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Azure module logged an Activity Log management-plane event: a Microsoft.Authorization/read / 'az account show' operation from data.srcip=10.108.57.184 against the corporate subscription. The call confirms the subscription id and tenant context — the opening move of an attacker validating stolen Azure credentials before acting. Wazuh fired a low-level Azure Activity Log rule for management-plane read access; on its own this is routine, but it is the first beat of a 4-step chain.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched an Azure Activity Log management-plane read ('account' / 'subscription' read) with agent.* identifying the Azure log-collector agent. The univariate signature is weak alone — it becomes meaningful only as step 1 of the chain that follows within 10 minutes.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Service Discovery", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 08:03:50 CEST (~2 minutes later), the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued a Microsoft.Web/sites/read operation — 'az functionapp list --resource-group rg-prod-corp' — enumerating every Azure Function app in the production resource group. This is attacker discovery: the actor is inventorying the serverless attack surface, looking for function apps to abuse or function endpoints with weak auth. Wazuh fired a medium-level rule on the Microsoft.Web/sites/read operation. The enumeration immediately follows the credential-validation step — a methodical operator workflow.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'Microsoft.Web/sites/read' / 'functionapp list' with the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 seen in step 1, ~2 minutes after step 1. The read of the entire production resource group's function inventory is the serverless-recon signature that precedes function abuse.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:03:50+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution — Serverless Execution", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 08:06:28 CEST, an inbound HTTP POST hit the anonymous function endpoint https://corpops-api.azurewebsites.net/api/HttpTrigger1 — the function's authLevel is 'anonymous', so NO API key is required and ANYONE on the internet can trigger it. App Insights / the Azure Function request telemetry recorded the inbound request, and Wazuh fired rule 92972 ('Azure Function anonymous invocation') at high severity, mapped to T1648 (Serverless Execution). The POST body carried a command-style JSON payload ({\"cmd\":\"id\",...}) consistent with an attacker probing whether the anonymous function can be coerced into running attacker-supplied logic. An anonymously-triggerable function is arbitrary attacker-controlled code execution inside the cloud tenant with no authentication barrier.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92972 fired with rule.description matching 'Azure Function anonymous invocation' and rule.mitre.id containing T1648. The inbound trigger correlates in time (within ~4 minutes) and intent with the step-2 functionapp enumeration: the attacker enumerated function apps, then triggered one whose authLevel was anonymous. This is the Execution beat of the chain.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:06:28+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution / Persistence — Serverless Execution, rogue function app creation", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 08:09:14 CEST, the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued a Microsoft.Web/sites/write operation — 'az functionapp create --name corp-helper-20260513 --resource-group rg-prod-corp --runtime python' — attempting to stand up a NEW, attacker-controlled Azure Function app in the production resource group. Wazuh fired rule 92973 ('Azure Function app creation by unexpected user') at critical severity, mapped to T1648. A function code stub was also dropped to /tmp on the agent host (Wazuh FIM target). Creating a rogue function app is the attacker establishing their own serverless execution + persistence foothold: an attacker-owned function runs code on demand inside the tenant, survives credential resets, and blends with legitimate platform activity. This is the chain's most damaging beat — it converts a discovery+execution incident into a persistence incident.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92973 fired with rule.description matching 'Azure Function app creation' / 'Microsoft.Web/sites/write' and rule.mitre.id containing T1648, with the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 driving every prior step. The create operation ~3 minutes after the anonymous trigger completes the chain: validate creds → enumerate functions → trigger an anonymous function → create a rogue function app for persistence.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:09:14+0200"}
  ],
